Description: Fix assert macro undeclared in abseil and perfetto headers
 The assert macro may be undefined by Chromium's base/logging.h or
 cef_logging.h headers which use #undef assert for their own DCHECK
 implementation. When abseil and perfetto headers include <cassert>
 before other includes, the assert macro gets undefined by subsequent
 Chromium header inclusions.
 .
 The fix is to re-include <assert.h> (NOT <cassert>) after all other
 includes in each affected header file. CRITICAL: We must use <assert.h>
 instead of <cassert> because:
 - <cassert> has a header guard (#ifndef _GLIBCXX_CASSERT) which prevents
   re-inclusion - if it was already included, it does nothing
 - <assert.h> is specifically designed to be re-includeable - it does
   #undef _ASSERT_H and #undef assert at the beginning before redefining
 .
 Affected files:
  - third_party/abseil-cpp/absl/strings/internal/str_format/bind.h
  - third_party/abseil-cpp/absl/strings/internal/str_format/constexpr_parser.h
  - third_party/perfetto/include/perfetto/protozero/scattered_stream_writer.h
  - third_party/perfetto/include/perfetto/tracing/internal/data_source_type.h
  - third_party/perfetto/include/perfetto/tracing/locked_handle.h
Author: Juan Manuel Méndez Rey <vejeta@gmail.com>
Bug-Debian: https://bugs.debian.org/915400
Forwarded: not-needed
Last-Update: 2025-12-07

